 
          <p>This blog post serves as a not so gentle reminder that I should practice what I preach: when I turned <a href="/post/2025/08/forty/">forty</a>, I wrote:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Once things settle down, I hope to finally start writing that own philosophical work.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Things didn&rsquo;t settle down. Perhaps that&rsquo;s the excuse. Still, compiling my own philosophy is still one of my main goals for this (and the next) year. I&rsquo;ve been working on it on (and more off) for a good year now. The theoretical framework is in place, the notebooks are filled. It&rsquo;s time to get serious. If Rousseau did it when he was forty, why can&rsquo;t I?</p>
<p>The lives of most of these French 17th, 18th, and even 19th century philosophers are filled with pain and misery. Montaigne got five kids but only one made it to adulthood. He himself did before he turned sixty. Pascal died half a year away from reaching forty. His sister Jacqueline barely made it to thirty-six. Descartes&mdash;died at 53&mdash;had a daughter while staying in The Netherlands who died at the age of five.</p>
<p>What do we do when we read about all that death? Panic? Montaigne famously suggested to build your house with windows facing the local cemetery. That way, you&rsquo;ll practice your personal <em>Memento Mori</em> sayings every single day. The years zip by without me noticing it. Writing the <em>Forty</em> blog post felt like yesterday yet here we are. The older I get the more obsessed I become with trying to &ldquo;live more fully&rdquo; by following the aforementioned philosophers in their footsteps.</p>
<p>But not too much. Montaigne wrote extensively about educating children yet left his only still alive child and wive to explore Germany and Italy for almost a year. Pascal hated upper class aristocratic distractions yet loved to mingle with them in the Paris salons to keep his fame. Rousseau vowed to take it easy and go back to nature, yet made his lover pregnant and ran away thinking he couldn&rsquo;t take care of them. Seneca suggests to enjoy the small things and live frugally yet was one of the richest statesman in ancient Rome. Heidegger proclaimed his hatred for technology that destroys the beauty of nature we no longer see, yet sided with the upcoming fascists that did nothing but employ technology to kill anything and everyone who didn&rsquo;t agree with their opinion.</p>
<p>Our son is one. We no longer have a tiny baby that requires constant attention. We now only have two whiners that require constant attention. He started eating little fruit pieces by himself. The mixer doesn&rsquo;t need to do its job anymore. I can&rsquo;t believe that intensive part of parenthood is already gone, even though there are still plenty of intensive parts left to tackle.</p>
<p>And yet, in the grand scheme of things, nothing really matters. According to the Existentialists, the cosmos hates you: we&rsquo;re but a speck of coincidental cosmic dust.</p>
<p>So there is little else to do but to accept the horrors of the gaping void and to forge our own destiny.</p>
<p>There. I guess I might as well have that other big piece of chocolate.</p>
<hr>
